<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crati</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Crathis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crati, the ancient Crathis, is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, flowing about 80 kilometres from the Sila mountains past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosenza">Cosenza</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>. The Greek colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a> stood on the coastal plain near its mouth. After Croton destroyed Sybaris in 510 BCE, the victors are said to have diverted the river over the ruins, which now lie under thick river deposits.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crati.jpg" alt="Crati" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DescriptionCrati (fleuve).png This map was created from OpenStreetMap project data, collected by the community. This ... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crati_(fleuve).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
